# Portfolio Summary: SpaceX Falcon 9 Landing Prediction

This project was completed as part of my master's capstone. It is a strong example of my work in data preparation, exploratory analysis, machine learning, and presentation of technical findings.

## What I Did

I built a predictive model to estimate whether a Falcon 9 first-stage booster would successfully land based on mission-level features. The project included:

- collecting launch data from public sources
- cleaning and standardizing missing or inconsistent values
- exploring relationships between mission variables and landing success
- engineering features for modeling
- comparing several classification models
- evaluating model performance using cross-validation and test accuracy
- summarizing findings through visualizations and analysis notes

## Technical Skills Demonstrated

- Python
- pandas and NumPy
- scikit-learn
- SQL-style analysis
- exploratory data analysis
- model evaluation and comparison
- data visualization
- dashboarding

## Project Results

The project notebooks report the following approximate performance:

- Logistic Regression: 84.64% cross-validation accuracy, 83.33% test accuracy
- SVM: 84.82% cross-validation accuracy, 83.33% test accuracy
- KNN: 84.82% cross-validation accuracy, 83.33% test accuracy
- Decision Tree: 83.33% test accuracy as reported in the notebook

Because the dataset is relatively small, the results should be interpreted with caution, but the project demonstrates a solid workflow and a clear understanding of classification modeling.

